Now I have all the test code and the extended catalog. Let me compile the full 19-smell audit with integration-test calibration applied.

---

## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · **.NET 10** · **Integration test** (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 2           | 2              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

**7 tests analyzed · 3 are smell-free ✓**

---

### Findings by Severity

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**Catalog name:** *Conditional Test Logic* (testsmells.org core smell)
**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` — line 94
**Rationale:** The `if`/`else` branch means one assertion path *never* runs per execution. Even in an integration suite, this hides a testing gap — you can't know from a green run whether the `PremiumUser` path or the fallback path was exercised.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — one branch is dead per run
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix — split into two deterministic tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "Vip", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com");

    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** The `PremiumUser` branch may *never* execute in CI, giving false confidence that discount-rate logic is tested.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Catalog name:** *Unknown Test / Empty Test* (testsmells.org core smell)
**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` — line 113
**Rationale:** This method exercises `Insert` 100 times but asserts nothing. MSTest reports it as passed even if every insert silently corrupts data. The name implies "doesn't throw" but that intent isn't expressed via an assertion.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — no assertion at all
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

**Fix — verify the side effects:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    const int count = 100;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(count, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False pass — 100 % coverage credit with zero behavioral verification.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Sleepy Test

**Catalog name:** *Sleepy Test* (testsmells.org core smell)
**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` — line 82
**Rationale:** `Thread.Sleep(3000)` adds 3 seconds of wall-clock time per run. If the notification mechanism becomes faster or slower, this test either wastes time or flakes. Additionally, `Thread.Sleep` is a synchronous blocking call inside an `async Task` method, which blocks the thread pool thread instead of yielding.

*Integration-test calibration:* Severity downgraded from High → **Medium**. Integration tests sometimes legitimately wait for async infrastructure, but a fixed 3-second sleep is still the wrong mechanism.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — hard-coded blocking sleep in async method
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Fix — poll with a timeout:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll up to 5 s, failing fast when the condition is met
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
    while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
    {
        Assert.IsTrue(DateTime.UtcNow < deadline, "Notification was not sent within timeout.");
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** 3 s wasted on every run + flaky failures when the system is slower than expected.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**Catalog name:** *Assertion Roulette* (testsmells.org extended smell)
**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` — line 28
**Rationale:** Four assertions with no message parameters. When one fails, the CI log shows `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>` with no context about *which* property was wrong.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — 4 bare assertions, no messages
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Fix — add message parameters:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be auto-assigned on insert.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure diagnosis in CI — developers must read source to locate which assertion failed.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

Three tests are **clean** and well-structured:

| Test | Why it's good |
|------|--------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, focused on one behavior, only 2 assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single assertion verifying absence — crisp negative test |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Properly `async`/`await`, asserts final count, clean concurrency pattern |

`ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` is also solid — `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` after adding 3 items is contextually obvious (not Magic Number).

---

### Smells Not Detected (15 of 19 clean) ✓

Mystery Guest, Eager Test, Magic Number Test, Sensitive Equality, Exception Handling, General Fixture, Ignored Test, Duplicate Assert, Lazy Test, Constructor Initialization, Default Test, Redundant Print, Redundant Assertion, Resource Optimism, Empty Test — **none found**.

The in-memory SQLite setup is hermetic (not Mystery Guest), and every fixture field is used by all tests (not General Fixture).

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** (`BulkInsert`) | Add `Assert.AreEqual(count, users.Count)` | ~2 min |
| 2 | **Conditional Test Logic** (`GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType`) | Split into 2 deterministic tests | ~10 min |
| 3 | **Sleepy Test** (`NotifyOnInsert`) | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll loop + timeout | ~10 min |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** (`InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase`) | Add message strings to 4 assertions | ~2 min |

Priorities 1 and 2 eliminate **false-pass risk** (tests that can report green when behavior is broken). Priority 3 reduces **flakiness and wall-clock time**. Priority 4 is a **diagnostic improvement**.
